# Agora — inventory & reservations (Sprint 06)
# Run against: dotnet run --project src/Agora.Api  (defaults to http://localhost:5158)
# Seed first:  dotnet run --project src/Agora.Api -- migrate seed
# VS Code REST Client / JetBrains HTTP client compatible.
#
# The seed gives every product a shelf, deliberately at different heights: a healthy middle, two
# products sitting on the low-stock threshold, and one genuinely sold out — so the low-stock view has
# rows in it and the storefront has an out-of-stock badge, immediately, without buying anything.

@host = http://localhost:5158

# ── What a shopper may have ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Availability. Note `availableAt`: the count is a read of a MOVING number, and the timestamp says
# so out loud. Between this response and a click, a reservation can lapse and another can be taken.
# The number was true when computed. That is not the same as being true now.
# @name stock
GET {{host}}/api/v1/products/{{productId}}/stock

### The sold-out one from the seed (AGA-KIT-002's neighbours vary; find one with available: 0).
GET {{host}}/api/v1/products?limit=12

# ── Holding stock ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Reserve. ANONYMOUS on purpose: a claim is held by a CART, and S05 established a cart may belong
# to a browser with no account. Require a session here and the last skillet becomes reservable only
# by people who already signed up — the shopper most likely to abandon is the only one who cannot
# hold what they are buying. The response mints agora_cart if this is your first write.
# @name reserve
POST {{host}}/api/v1/cart/reservations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 1
}

### Reserve MORE than exists → 200 with success:false and the number that stopped you.
# 🔍 Not a 4xx. The request was well formed and the answer is simply no — "you cannot have nine of
# these" is Tuesday, not an error. And a UI needs the NUMBER: "only 3 left" is a sentence a shopper
# can act on; a 409 with the count buried in a `detail` string is a number somebody parses out of
# prose. Compare with the next request: two different kinds of no, two different shapes.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/cart/reservations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 9999
}

### Reserve a product that does not exist → 404. THIS is the other kind of no: you asked about
# nothing. (Since S06, every product has a shelf — so "no stock row" genuinely means "no product",
# and this 404 is true rather than merely usual.)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/cart/reservations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
  "quantity": 1
}

### Reserve twice from the same cart → the claim is REPLACED, not stacked. Ask for 2, then 3: you
# end up holding 3, not 5. Count a shopper's own claim against them and they are refused stock the
# shop is holding on their behalf — unbuyable by them, unavailable to anyone else, until it expires.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/cart/reservations
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 3
}

### Give it all back (the checkout finished, or the shopper gave up).
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/cart/reservations

# ── Watch expiry happen with nothing running ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# TRY: reserve the last unit of something, then check availability — it is 0. Now wait fifteen
# minutes and check again. It is 1, and NOTHING RAN: no job, no sweep, no notification. The window
# passed, so IsActiveAt(now) went false, so availability stopped counting it. That is the entire
# mechanism.
#
# The sweep (ReleaseExpiredReservations) is a JANITOR that marks rows which already stopped
# mattering — it keeps queries small and the table legible. Switch it off for a week and the shop is
# correct and untidy. A background job whose failure is a mess rather than an outage is the one to
# aim for; the alternative (availability reads a counter only the sweep decrements) turns a stuck
# job into silent overselling.

# ── The shop's own view ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Sign in as the Store Admin (dev creds are public on purpose — see AgoraSeeder).
# @name signIn
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@agora.test",
  "password": "admin-password-dev"
}

### Running low. THREE numbers per row, not one: "2 available" is an emergency if two are on the
# shelf and a non-event if nine are on the shelf with seven in people's carts. Same figure, opposite
# actions — and a report showing only availability sends someone to the stockroom to count skillets
# that are already there.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/admin/stock/low

### A different threshold — it is a query parameter, not a business rule. The shop discovers its own
# number by using the thing.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/admin/stock/low?threshold=10

### As OPS. The first endpoint in Agora that two roles reach: Ops packs the boxes, so Ops is exactly
# who needs to know what is running out — and still may not touch a price. That is "roles are
# capabilities, not a hierarchy" (S02) finally costing something.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "ops@agora.test",
  "password": "ops-password-dev"
}

###
GET {{host}}/api/v1/admin/stock/low

### As a CUSTOMER → 403. Not 401: we know exactly who you are, and the answer is still no.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "shopper@agora.test",
  "password": "shopper-password-dev"
}

###
GET {{host}}/api/v1/admin/stock/low
